※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4569

※ A generic function is written once and compiled once per set of types, and
※ which sets those are is what the calls turn out to ask for.  Named where a
※ value is wanted there is no call to ask, so there is no one function for the
※ name to stand for and no type for it to have.
※
※ What can be handed over is a function whose signature is settled, which a
※ generic becomes at each instantiation -- but an instantiation is made by a
※ call, and this is not one.

fn pick(a: T', b: T') → T':
    a

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let f: fn(u8, u8) → u8 = pick
    if f(1u8, 2u8) ≠ 1u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
